detection. Cutting it simultaneously is unrelated risk. Payload pruning can happen producer-by-producer later.\n\nYour example also contains two semantic errors:\n\n- `04:13Z` in Madrid during August is `06:13+02:00`, not `04:13+02:00`.\n- It must say “Message received,” not “Current time.” “Current time” becomes false immediately upon replay.\n\n## Legacy behavior\n\nFor existing data-only `v: 1` message parts: your proposed loss policy is coherent. No backfill; new readers ignore them. Previous `runs.context_items` records still retain what past Runs actually received.\n\nBut compaction is different. [ContextBuilder](/home/leon0399/.herdr/worktrees/llame/chat-page-title/apps/api/src/chats/context-builder.ts:130) regenerates the entire checkpoint envelope from `compactions.summary`. Dropping a legacy checkpoint would erase the summarized conversation history from model context, not merely remove an old reminder.\n\nRecommendation:\n\n- New compactions persist both raw `summary` and immutable full model-facing checkpoint text.\n- Legacy compactions continue through the old renderer until superseded by a later compaction.\n- No backfill.\n- Document legacy checkpoint replay as the only pre-cutover exception.\n\n## Remaining limitation\n\nConfidence: high. This fixes reminder stability, not absolute provider-prefix stability. User text sanitization, sender attribution, tool-result projection, compaction handling, SDK serialization, and provider adapters can still alter historical wire input across releases.\n\nThe honest guarantee is:\n\n> Persisted context-item blocks created after the cutover replay with identical application-level text and block order.\n\nNot:\n\n> The entire provider request remains byte-identical forever.\n\nThere is no active OpenSpec change, and I made no edits. The one contract decision needed before proposing this is whether legacy compaction checkpoints get the recommended fallback or are deliberately discarded with legacy message reminders.